tero*. That connection is severed at birth. There is no natural mechanism for a newborn to receive oxygen directly from water through its skin or any other organ. Even if a baby had some magical ability to stay underwater, their body temperature would drop dangerously, and they would suffer severe hypothermia without the warmth of the womb and the mother’s body. ### In summary A baby can safely remain submerged for a few minutes after a water birth **only because** it is still receiving oxygen from the placenta and the dive reflex prevents inhalation. Once that cord stops supplying oxygen, the instinct to breathe overrides everything. If the baby is still underwater, they will inhale water, leading to drowning. Humans simply don’t have the biology to extract oxygen from water—that’s why we can’t stay underwater like a mermaid.
