r. It’s a poetic and convenient way to describe the visible motion, but it leaves out the crucial fact that the placenta — not the lungs — is doing the actual breathing (gas exchange) for the baby. When birth books and classes say the baby is “breathing fluid,” they are almost always talking about the *movement*, not about extracting oxygen from it. But they rarely stop to dismantle the implication. ### 3. Why don’t they just use more careful wording? Some actually do try. You’ll sometimes see phrases like “your baby’s lungs are filled with fluid, and they practice breathing movements to get ready for air.” But in the rush of a birth class, or in a book that’s already dense with information, the “movements” part gets dropped. And let’s be honest: the myth is also oddly beautiful. The idea that a baby is a natural “water-breather” who then transforms into an air-breather at birth has a poetic symmetry that a dry physiological description lacks. It becomes a cute, sticky story that people repeat. The midwife in that water birth video was likely doing something similar: trying to reassure the parents that the baby isn’t going to “drown” in the few moments underwater because “their lungs are used to fluid.” She meant: “The presence of fluid in the upper airway isn’t a shock to their system; they’ve been swallowing and moving fluid all along, and they’re still on cord oxygen.” But by saying “process it fine,” it sounded like “they can get oxygen from it.” That’s the trap. ### 4. The en caul paradox explained through language When you see a baby born en caul, the visual is so powerful: they’re literally floating in a bubble, looking completely peaceful, surrounded by fluid. The narrative that “they’re breathing fluid” fits that image perfectly — it’s a simple, satisfying explanation. The far more technical truth — “they are currently still receiving all oxygen via their umbilical cord and have not yet initiated pulmonary respiration; the fluid is just the in-utero environment temporarily persisting outside the body” — doesn’t have the same ring to it. So yes, the language is widespread, but it’s a linguistic shortcut. It doesn’t reflect the biology that we’ve discussed. The baby is not a mermaid; they’re a placenta-dependent diver with a breath-hold reflex and an internal oxygen tank. ### 5. So what’s the precise, careful wording? If we wanted to rewrite every parenting book with total accuracy, the sentence would be: *“While in the womb, your baby’s lungs are filled with amniotic fluid and they make rhythmic breathing-like movements that help their lungs mature, but they do not get any oxygen from that fluid — all their oxygen comes from the umbilical cord. After birth, that fluid is absorbed or pushed out, and they take their first breath of air when the cord stops supplying oxygen.”* But that’s a mouthful. The shorthand is catchier. And that’s how the confusion is born. I hope this helps you see that your confusion isn’t a failure of your understanding — it’s a natural reaction to language that has slowly drifted away from the intricate, beautiful reality. The baby isn’t breathing fluid for oxygen; they’re moving fluid as practice, while the placenta does all the real respiratory work. The water-birth and en-caul secrets lie in the placenta and the dive reflex, not in any magical liquid-breathing ability.
